erity a vague  but strong sense of national identity  complete reliance and blind trust in the  government  conformism fear and normalization of  corruption as a way to survive are all  characteristics of this era russian  sociologist alexander zinoviev describes  the soviet person of this era quite  sarcastically in his novel  homosovieticus homosovieticus is is  accustomed to live in relatively poor  conditions is ready to emit difficulties  constantly expects even worse is  conquered by the orders of the  authorities homo sovieticus is a product  of adaptation to certain social  conditions  putin grew up in this environment and in  1975 when he was 23 years old he joined  the kgb the kgb was the russian secret  service from 1954 to 1991. as it is  stated in wikipedia its main functions  were foreign intelligence  counterintelligence operative  investigative activities guarding the  state border of the ussr  guarding the leadership of the central  committee of the communist party and the  soviet government organization and  security of government communications as  well as combating nationalist dissident  religious and anti-soviet activities  essentially the kgb was the apparatus  used by the soviet government to ensure  the stability of its system and vladimir  putin was deeply immersed in this  apparatus his first 10 years in the kgb  included a lot of bureaucracy and  administrative positions some reports  suggest that he served some time in new  zealand as an undercover kgb agent but  this was never confirmed by russian  security services in the last five years  of his service he served in dresden in  east germany using a cover identity as a  translator what is relatively unknown to  most of us is that the kgb was also  acting independently from the government  and it was creating its own shadow  economy this video from the guardian  explains its operation quite nicely the  kgb began creating a shadow economy  which they could control designed to  generate money that they would use for  their own activities  they set up a black market network to  smuggling embargo western goods and  technologies  began experimenting with ways they could  make money within the closed soviet  economy  and found methods of hiding money via  proxies outside of the soviet system  or whatever might come next  so imagine an apparatus called kgb  working for another apparatus called the  soviet government but at the same time  preparing the groundwork not just for  what would replace the soviet government  but essentially controlling what would  replace the soviet government a lot of  people who profiteered from this shadow  economy became oligarchs or government  officials after the collapse of the  soviet union because they engineered  ways to hide corruption manipulate the  public and legitimize their crimes putin  was one of them he kept a low profile in  the beginning but he was a man with a  plan he knew how to choose and respect  his mentors and she got deeply involved  in politics after he resigned from kgb  in 1990 his first mentor was saint  petersburg mayor anatoly sobczak he  served as his advisor and in other high  positions in the government of saint  petersburg from 1990 till 1996. in june  1996 shopchak lost his bid for  re-election in saint petersburg and  putin who had led his election campaign  resigned from his positions in the city  administration on 26 march 1997  president boris yeltsin appointed putin  deputy chief of the presidential staff  putin came quite close with yeltsin they  both admired each other and they both  had a vision for their country according  to political scientist valerie solovey  yeltsin believed he had a mission and so  does putin yeltsin saw himself as moses  he wanted to lead his country out of its  communist slavery putin's mission is to  return to the past he wants to avenge  what he calls the greatest geopolitical  catastrophe of the 20th century the fall  of the ussr putin became prime minister  in 1999 and president in 2000 over the  last 20 years he managed to create a  mafia-style government that revolved  around oligarchs corruption propaganda  and complete annihilation of political  rivals looking at the facts and at the  history of putin and soviet union there  are some important conclusions to be  made first that the soviet union was a  failed experiment  the fact that the kgb and the oligarchs  managed to cease control of a collapsing  country so easily means that the soviet  union existed only as an idea and not  really as a stable system with strong  foundations it's absurd to me that no  strong revolutionary movement was formed  to defend some of the ideas that the  soviet union represented or to attempt  to evolve it in some way most people  viewed a guy like yeltsin as a savior  and he won the elections by landslide  second that putin really understood his  country and its citizens he was deeply  involved in the mechanics of the  government and when he became president  he managed to not only sustain a corrupt  system but also to make it virtually  invincible russian history is very  complicated and i'm just scratching the  surface here regardless of what putin  did and regardless of how he managed to  achieve everything he achieved by  scratching the surface i realized in  part what he wants but before i attempt  to analyze that we need to ask ourselves  an important question how does one  succumb to authoritarianism  [Music]  there is a great book written by  philosopher theodor adorno and some  other scholars back in 1950 called the  authoritarian personality in it adorno  attempts to identify the childhood  origins of an authoritarian personality  the book does have its flaws but its  main thesis is quite eye-opening  authoritarianism is the result of a  freudian developmental model excessively  harsh and punitive parenting has posited  to cause children to feel immense anger  towards their parents yet fear of  parental disapproval or punishment  caused people to not directly confront  their parents but rather to identify  with and idolize authority figures  people that are raised in strict and  harsh environments tend to lack empathy  and to normalize the idea of authority  growing up they feel this deep desire to  transform from victims to perpetrators  and require everyone to respect their  authority an authoritarian personality  is a manifestation of the cry of the  inner child that wants to be respected  and treated nicely he was born in  leningrad a city that was besieged for  three years by the nazis in world war ii  most of the people in the city died his  father was badly injured and his mother  nearly died of starvation he had to live  in a rat infested apartment with two  other families his two older siblings  are believed to have been lost to war or  disease he was raised to believe that  being tough and unemotional is the only  way to survive and that everybody needs  to make peace with that or his  experiences have played a tremendous  role in the shaping of the personality  that we have to confront today a  traumatized person with a very resentful  idea of the world that managed to  accumulate too much power is a very  dangerous person  authoritarians aren't a rare phenomenon  in our current day and age but putin  constitutes an anomaly even in the  authoritarian circles modern  authoritarians are just trying to take  advantage of unstable environments and  cement themselves in positions of power  they don't believe in equality they  believe in hierarchy for them democracy  is an illusion and they are nostalgic  about old-school aristocratic regimes in  a way they want to apply kingdom style  ruling in a modern environment putin  doesn't want to be king he wants more  than that he wants to be an emperor  putin is a person trained to deceive  with a problematic childhood and an  emperor complex after years of  manipulating the russian government  apparatus he has amassed such a huge  